# Byte stability across the Windows dev box and the Linux/ROCm deploy host.
#
# This project is deliberately hardware-portable: development runs on Windows
# (RTX 4090, autocrlf=true) and deployment runs on Ubuntu (ROCm). Several
# tracked files are hashed BYTE-FOR-BYTE and compared across hosts:
#
#   - the corpus fixture, whose contentHash feeds datasetHash
#   - every model record mirrored into models/ (manifests, provenance,
#     train-state, config), re-checked by store.verify_mirror
#   - the tokenizer (tokenizerHash)
#
# If the Windows checkout silently converted those files to CRLF, the bytes
# would differ from the Linux host and from the authoritative store, and a
# mirror check would (correctly) report drift that is really just a line-ending
# artifact. Normalize text to LF in the working tree on every platform so the
# hash is a function of content alone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Weight and optimizer state are content-addressed binary blobs. Never
# line-ending-convert or text-diff them. (They are gitignored from the tree, but
# a mis-staged or exported copy must not be silently mangled.)
*.safetensors binary
*.pt binary
*.bin binary
*.gguf binary

# Keep the git-tracked record set unambiguously text even if a future file is
# empty or has no detectable content, so it can never fall back to CRLF.
models/** text eol=lf
models/**/COMMITTED text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
